 it is sad to witness  this but no character can constantly go  on within the story without making  mistakes especially since actions have  consequences and my rock following his  heart and marrying to lissa he doomed  himself  so even though the conflict isn't as  drastic as season 2 season 3 is still  able to create engaging conflict without  any form of reliance on action  a big reason why season 2 may feel like  one of the weaker seasons of the first  four is that jon and daenerys didn't  have that great of a story however now  that they have found their footing more  as characters links to season two they  are able to have much more fun and  progress in a more satisfying manner and  by this i mean you probably weren't that  engaged by danny being stuck in qarth  and not really being able to get closer  to her overall goal of building an army  and getting to westeros season 3  perfectly satisfies this and george  achieves this in such a spectacular  fashion that the payoff feels orgasmic  danny getting the unsullied is easily  one of the best scenes because of the  sheer payoff for three seasons of  everyone waiting for dany to pop off on  someone and accumulate more power and  this isn't just good because it's  satisfying payoff but it's also written  expertly in which freeing the slaves  ties into daenerys overall goal of  liberating and freeing everyone held  under tyranny so there's subtext under  this twist and it's also great how the  audience wasn't aware of dany's plan to  make this development even more  surprising  through the course of season 3 for dany  there's also a major injection of more  characters which was much needed because  the only dynamic we really had before  this was with sir jorah and his dynamic  with dany is great it's just that you  need to offer up more characters so that  you can have a larger variety of  interactions like we end up getting sir  barris and selmy missandei greyworm and  daario to spruce things up sir barristan  offers a great perspective given that he  served danny's father and any  interaction he has with her is  insightful on top of it also being  wholesome missandei offers a much-needed  casual avenue that dany can utilize  since every interaction she has is  normally tied to her conquest grey worm  may seem like the most dull out of these  characters but he can still be pretty  cute considering that his surrounding  characters tend to dismantle his more  professional demeanor then lastly we  have daario who acts as a love interest  and he doesn't just add that but that  introduces a conflict and what sergiora  also loves dany despite him not being  able to pursue that so dario's presence  is like constant terminal for portal sir  jorah all of these characters makes the  dialogue encounter surrounding dany much  more complex and varied and it's  something i think most people may  overlook  then we don't just have a major  improvement for daenerys but we also  have jon snow getting a major upgrade in  the last season analysis i argued that  his journey beyond the wall felt  lackluster because he didn't have much  to do and now that season three  submerged within the free folk it makes  every scene he's in utterly entrancing  because the story can go in any  direction and the stakes are really high  will jon be discovered as a spy will he  grow more sympathetic towards their  cause and will he end up falling in love  with egret or will he uphold his vows to  the night's watch these developments are  already significantly more than what  happened in season 2 where it felt like  he was idly waiting for something big to  happen then this also thoroughly changes  jon as a character as his entire outlook  on the wildlings changes i love the  opening scene with mance where at first  jon is trying to give some [ __ ]  response as to why he wants to join them  but then he digs deeper for why he  really should join them thousands of  years ago  the first men battled the white walkers  and defeated them  i want to fight for the side  the fights for the living  did i come to the right place  this singular moment establishes a great  internal conflict for jon of wanting to  help their free folk while also not  wanting to kill his brothers something  that is explored more later on once they  go over the wall it's a perfect seat  that's planted that leads to an  outstanding resolution through the  watchers on the wall and hard home  to me this is why john's season three  through five journey is by far his peak  in the show because when he's being torn  apart between his love of egret and the  duty to his brothers and makes for an  amazing conflict especially since it was  set up in season one with mr amon's  quotes love is the death of duty so when  we go from a great internal struggle  interlaced with an equally good plot to  the later seasons where he basically  just becomes a hollow drone where he  only cares about beating the night king  you can see a clear drop off and it's  also ironic that jon's internal conflict  with a free folk in the night's watch  somehow manages to greatly surpass his  targaryen identity crisis in season  eight thanks to goodall david and dan  i think in season three the crazy  lannister family dynamic was at its best  just barely beating out season four  because tyrion was kind of locked away  for the whole season now we are  technically missing jaime in season 3  for the king's landing story but i think  the dynamic between tyrion cersei and  tywin is practically perfect and is  present throughout the entirety of the  season and it's not like jaime isn't  being well utilized this season given  that he has his all-time best scene with  brienne where he spells the truth about  the mad king but going back to king's  landing the beauty of it is that it's a  capital which so many characters  intertwine and have a chance to interact  with each other am i bringing in a  collection of rich and complex  characters that have layers of  backgrounds with each other is going to  lead to outstanding dialogue scenes  influence grows like a weed  any man who must say i am the king is no  true king  chaos is a ladder  in the first episode alone we have one  of tyrion and tywin's best scenes  together in which tywin is being  truthful about how he feels about tyrion  it's utterly tragic and the once  confident tyrion is reduced to a scared  child as his father castrates him  verbally you are an ill-made spiteful  little creature  full of envy lust and low cunning but  neither gods nor men will ever compel me  to let you turn casterly rock into your  [ __ ] house and this is all after turn  is just looking for some praise from his  heroic acts in the battle of the black  water  to me taiwan and tyrion is the best  rivalry in the show because of the pure  conflict between them a father that  fundamentally hates his child while that  child desperately craves his approval  and instead constantly gets beaten down  by him and it also makes it even better  that these are the two best characters  in the show and talon in particular is  the most powerful man alive so having an  intimate relationship with this figure  is infinitely intriguing i'm kind of  gushing about tyrion and tywin right now  and there's also you know another  magnificent character that's included in  this dynamic which is cersei a character  who was once dominant in king's landing  for the first two seasons is put in  check by her father is definitely an  interesting change of pace for her  especially during the explosive scene  where tywin's so fed up with his  children and not wanting to comply with  the tyrells that he screams at them and  asserts his chad dominance you're my  daughter  you will do as i command and you will  marry loras tyrell  this also brings up a point of relation  since tyrion and cersei are both  subjected to their father so they stop  fighting each other and grow a bit  closer their scenes together this season  are so moving especially since tyrion  can even relate to the fact that now  he's starting a quote-unquote family  with sansa so he can kind of relate to  cersei's family drama this unlocks that  rare dialogue where cersei opens up  about her kids and becomes more warm and  intimate with tyrion and i find these  types of moments extremely juicy  how happy would you say you are  not very  but if it weren't for my children  i'd have thrown myself from the highest  window in the red keep  so although for season three there isn't  a major plot driven device like an  upcoming war or a sprawling murder  mystery to inherently up the excitement  factor i think the season is still able  to compete with the others because of  how it's still able to be extremely  engaging with its complex characters  hashing out intimate issues  after season one of game of thrones you  think wow george r martin just played  the biggest uno reverse card in cinema  history by killing the main protagonist  and there is no way it'll happen again  oh no no no pepe left we were so naive  going into season three the red wedding  is definitely the best scene in the show  and i've talked about it a lot so have  other people however i can't make a  season three analysis without going into  it now to throw the obvious things out  first the technical makeup of the scene  is perfect from the acting the writing  delivery pace tone everything about the  scene is executed at such an amazing  degree for me the personal standout is  michelle farrelly's performance as  catelyn and her extreme reaction to the  death of her son exudes a haunting layer  of dread and shock that traumatizes  everyone in the audience thanks to  george r martin making such an expansive  and sprawling story with dozens of major  characters he can do something like this  and not completely damage the story  beyond repair you look at any other show  and the cast of characters is normally  just a handful and because of that fact  you can't do anything major with them  without ruining the story this is why  game of thrones is special with such a  huge cast georgia martin can play with  the set foundations of storytelling and  make it seem like certain characters are  going to be the main character only to  swipe out the rug this makes every  character have an are of danger and  suspense around them because we don't  know how safe they are and whether the  story will continue for them this is why  i think the later seasons of the show  really fall off not to mention the  writing because once you get a set  number of main characters that you know  will make it to the end you can slowly  start to see their plot armor and any  danger around them evaporates making the  show have less tension the red wedding  is a major reminder of danger and  doesn't just impact one storyline but  makes the viewer worry about everyone so  not only is the scene in achievement and  storytelling it causes a massive ripple  effect and how you view the show as a  whole making it a complete masterpiece  so yeah those four unique reasons are  why i think season 3 stands out and is  amazing thank you guys so much for  watching through this series and if you  haven't seen my other season analysis  videos then you can check them out in  the end screen playlist and since it's  also been two years since i started this  series i feel like i drastically changed  so it may be a bit drawing going back to  some of my earlier video essays but  regardless have a great day
 

